 {id:'odd-sums',k:'rOdd',name:'Odd sums make squares',icon:'L',subtitle:'Each odd number adds an L-shaped border.',accent:'#ffb956',rgb:'255,185,86',preview:4,answer:25,options:[20,25,24],watch:'Watch borders of 1, 3, 5 and 7 dots build larger squares.',caption:'Each colour is one odd-number group. Separate the borders to inspect them.',prompt:'What is 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 9?',hint:'Five odd-number groups build a square of side 5.',question:'Why does adding the first n odd numbers give n²?',reasons:['Each odd border adds 1 to the side.','Each group adds exactly 2 dots.','All the groups have the same size.'],correct:0,why:'The first n odd numbers form n nested L-shaped groups filling an n × n square. Their sum is n².',success:'Five odd-number groups fill a 5 × 5 square: 25.'},
 {id:'up-and-down',k:'rUp',name:'Up and down → squares',icon:'↗↘',subtitle:'Count up to a peak, then back down.',accent:'#78c8f7',rgb:'120,200,247',preview:4,answer:25,options:[20,30,25],watch:'Follow the coloured diagonals. Their lengths rise, then fall.',caption:'A square has diagonal lengths 1, 2, …, n, …, 2, 1.',prompt:'What is 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 1?',hint:'The peak is 5. The picture is a square of side 5.',question:'What explains the up-and-down sum?',reasons:['There are n dots in every diagonal.','Its diagonals fill an n × n square.','The middle number is counted twice.'],correct:1,why:'The diagonals of an n × n square contain 1, 2, …, n, …, 2, 1 dots. The peak n is counted only once, and the total is n².',success:'Up to 5 and back down to 1 fills 25 places.'},
 {id:'ones-to-counting',k:'rOnes',name:'Adding 1s → counting',icon:'+1',subtitle:'One more 1. One more in the total.',accent:'#69d992',rgb:'105,217,146',preview:4,answer:5,options:[4,5,6],watch:'Add one unit at a time and read the running totals.',caption:'1; 1 + 1; 1 + 1 + 1; … give 1, 2, 3, …',prompt:'What is the sum of five 1s?',hint:'Count the five unit dots.',question:'Which sequence comes from adding more and more 1s?',reasons:['Square numbers.','Powers of 2.','Counting numbers.'],correct:2,why:'Adding n copies of 1 gives n. The running totals are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, …',success:'Five 1s give 5. The totals are counting numbers.'},
 {id:'ones-up-and-down',k:'rOnesUp',name:'1s up and down → odds',icon:'2n−1',subtitle:'One central 1, with equal wings.',accent:'#e998cd',rgb:'233,152,205',preview:4,answer:9,options:[9,10,11],watch:'At step n, use n ones going up and n − 1 coming down.',caption:'Count the peak once: n + (n − 1) = 2n − 1.',prompt:'Use five 1s on the way up and four on the way down. Total?',hint:'There are 5 + 4 ones, with the middle position counted once.',question:'Why do these totals stay odd?',reasons:['There are n + (n − 1) units: pairs and one extra.','There are n + n units in every picture.','Each 1 doubles at the next step.'],correct:0,why:'The peak is included only once. So the totals are 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, …, each equal to 2n − 1.',success:'5 + 4 = 9. Four pairs and one central unit.'},
 {id:'counting-to-triangles',k:'rTri',name:'Counting sums → triangles',icon:'△',subtitle:'Stack the next counting number as a row.',accent:'#e882b4',rgb:'232,130,180',preview:4,answer:15,options:[10,15,25],watch:'A row of 1, then 2, then 3: watch the triangle grow.',caption:'The totals 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, … are triangular numbers.',prompt:'What is 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5?',hint:'The first four rows contain 10 dots. Add a row of 5.',question:'Why does adding counting numbers produce triangles?',reasons:['Each new row has exactly 3 dots.','The rows have lengths 1, 2, 3, …, n.','The picture always has n² dots.'],correct:1,why:'Rows of 1, 2, 3, …, n dots form a filled triangle. Their sum is the nth triangular number, n(n + 1) ÷ 2.',success:'The five rows contain 15 dots altogether.'},
 {id:'triangle-pairs',k:'rPair',name:'Two triangles → a square',icon:'△+△',subtitle:'Fit consecutive triangles together.',accent:'#70d6cc',rgb:'112,214,204',preview:4,answer:36,options:[30,35,36],watch:'Separate the two colours. One triangle has one more row.',caption:'1 + 3 = 4; 3 + 6 = 9; 6 + 10 = 16; …',prompt:'What is 15 + 21, the next pair of triangular numbers?',hint:'Triangles with 5 and 6 rows fit into a 6 × 6 square.',question:'Why do consecutive triangular numbers add to a square?',reasons:['The triangles have equal totals.','Their totals differ by only 1.','They fill one square with no gaps.'],correct:2,why:'Tₙ + Tₙ₋₁ = n². One triangle includes the diagonal of the square; the other fills the remaining spaces.',success:'15 + 21 = 36. The two triangles fill one square.'},
 {id:'powers-two-sums',k:'rPowers',name:'One short of doubling',icon:'+1',subtitle:'Add powers of 2, then fill the missing place.',accent:'#8cb8ff',rgb:'140,184,255',preview:4,answer:31,options:[31,32,30],watch:'Coloured groups grow 1, 2, 4, 8, … One empty place completes the next power of 2.',caption:'The sums are 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, … Add 1 to get 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, …',prompt:'What is 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16, before adding the extra 1?',hint:'The groups fill all but one place in a collection of 32.',question:'What happens if we add 1 to each running sum?',reasons:['We get the next power of 2.','We get the odd-number sequence.','We get triangular numbers.'],correct:0,why:'1 + 2 + … + 2ⁿ⁻¹ = 2ⁿ − 1. Adding the missing 1 gives 2ⁿ, the next power of 2.',success:'The sum is 31. One extra unit completes 32.'},
 {id:'six-triangles',k:'rHex',name:'Six triangles + a centre',icon:'⬡',subtitle:'Arrange six triangular groups around one dot.',accent:'#ffc275',rgb:'255,194,117',preview:4,answer:91,options:[90,91,96],watch:'The six colours are equal triangular groups. Keep the centre separate.',caption:'6 × 1 + 1 = 7; 6 × 3 + 1 = 19; 6 × 6 + 1 = 37; …',prompt:'If each triangle has 15 dots, what is 6 × 15 + 1?',hint:'Six groups contain 90 dots. Include the centre dot.',question:'Which sequence do these totals belong to?',reasons:['Ordinary multiples of 6.','Hexagonal numbers: 1, 7, 19, 37, …','Square numbers: 1, 4, 9, 16, …'],correct:1,why:'Six copies of a triangular number, plus one centre dot, make 7, 19, 37, 61, 91, … These continue the centred hexagonal sequence that starts at 1.',success:'Six triangles of 15, plus the centre, make 91.'},
